.TH WORDSWIFT.SERVICE "5" "October 2026" "WordSwift 0.2.0" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
wordswift.service \- systemd unit for the WordSwift search engine
.SH SYNOPSIS
/usr/lib/systemd/system/wordswift.service
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBwordswift.service\fR runs the WordSwift HTTP API, a semantic word search
engine with full-text BM25, typo-tolerant fuzzy search and prefix completion
behind a MeiliSearch-compatible REST interface.
.PP
The unit is installed by the \fBwordswift\fR package and is enabled and started
automatically on a fresh install, and restarted on upgrade. It listens on
\fB127.0.0.1:7700\fR by default.
.PP
The process runs as the unprivileged \fBwordswift\fR system user, which is
created by \fI/usr/lib/sysusers.d/wordswift.conf\fR. Its state directory
\fI/var/lib/wordswift\fR is created by \fI/usr/lib/tmpfiles.d/wordswift.conf\fR
and by the unit's \fBStateDirectory=\fR directive, both of which set ownership to
that user. The directory is not shipped inside the package, because dpkg would
otherwise create it root-owned and the service user could not write indexes to it.
.SH UNIT SETTINGS
.TP
.B Type=simple
The process stays in the foreground.
.TP
.B User=wordswift, Group=wordswift
Runs unprivileged.
.TP
.B StateDirectory=wordswift
systemd creates \fI/var/lib/wordswift\fR, chowns it to the service user and makes
it writable before \fBExecStart\fR runs, and removes it on
\fBsystemctl clean\fR.
.TP
.B StateDirectoryMode=0750
Private to the service user and group.
.TP
.B ExecStart
\fI/usr/bin/wordswift serve --host ${WORDSWIFT_HOST} --port ${WORDSWIFT_PORT}
--data-dir /var/lib/wordswift\fR
.TP
.B EnvironmentFile=-/etc/wordswift/wordswift.env
Optional site overrides. The leading \fB-\fR makes a missing file non-fatal.
.TP
.B Restart=on-failure, RestartSec=5s
.TP
.B KillMode=process, TimeoutStopSec=30s
See \fBSHUTDOWN BEHAVIOUR\fR below.
.TP
.B NoNewPrivileges, PrivateTmp, ProtectSystem=full, ProtectHome
Sandboxing. Additionally \fBProtectKernelTunables\fR,
\fBProtectKernelModules\fR, \fBProtectControlGroups\fR, \fBRestrictSUIDSGID\fR,
\fBRestrictNamespaces\fR, \fBLockPersonality\fR, \fBProtectHostname\fR,
\fBProtectClock\fR and \fBRestrictRealtime\fR are enabled,
\fBCapabilityBoundingSet\fR is empty, \fBSystemCallFilter\fR allows
\fB@system-service\fR while denying \fB@privileged\fR and \fB@resources\fR, and
\fBSystemCallArchitectures=native\fR.
.TP
.B UMask=0027
New index files are 0640 and directories 0750, so catalog content is not
world-readable.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Only \fBWORDSWIFT_DATA_DIR\fR is read by the WordSwift application itself.
.PP
\fBWORDSWIFT_HOST\fR and \fBWORDSWIFT_PORT\fR are substituted into
\fBExecStart\fR by systemd and are \fInot\fR read by the Python code. Both are
given defaults in the unit with \fBEnvironment=\fR, because systemd expands an
undefined \fB${VAR}\fR to an empty string, which would pass empty arguments.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
\fI/etc/wordswift/wordswift.env\fR is a registered conffile, so local edits
survive package upgrades. It uses systemd \fBEnvironmentFile\fR syntax, which is
\fInot\fR shell syntax:
.RS 4
.nf
WORDSWIFT_PORT=8080          # correct
export WORDSWIFT_PORT=8080   # wrong, "export" is taken literally
WORDSWIFT_PORT="8080"        # wrong, quotes become part of the value
.fi
.RE
.PP
Apply changes with:
.RS 4
.nf
systemctl daemon-reload && systemctl restart wordswift
.fi
.RE
.PP
Note that \fBExecStart\fR passes \fB--data-dir\fR explicitly, so editing
\fBWORDSWIFT_DATA_DIR\fR alone does not relocate the indexes; override the unit
with \fBsystemctl edit wordswift.service\fR instead.
.SH SHUTDOWN BEHAVIOUR
\fBKillMode=process\fR is deliberate. LevelDB indexes are directories holding an
\fBexclusive\fR lock. systemd's default \fBcontrol-group\fR mode signals every
process in the cgroup and follows up with \fBSIGKILL\fR after the timeout; a
\fBSIGKILL\fRed server cannot close its LevelDB handles, which leaves a stale
\fBLOCK\fR file and makes the next start fail with:
.RS 4
.nf
IO error: lock /var/lib/wordswift/<index>_ft/LOCK:
Resource temporarily unavailable
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBKillMode=process\fR signals only the main process and lets it release the locks
cleanly.
.SH SINGLE-WRITER CONSTRAINT
Only one process may use a given data directory at a time, because LevelDB takes
an exclusive lock. Running a foreground server against the live state directory
while the unit is active fails with the lock error above. Stop the service first:
.RS 4
.nf
systemctl stop wordswift
wordswift serve --data-dir /var/lib/wordswift
.fi
.RE
.PP
This failure is intentionally loud. Earlier versions silently fell back to an
empty in-memory index, which served blank results and looked like data loss.
.SH SECURITY
WordSwift has \fBno built-in authentication\fR and enables CORS for all origins,
so the unit binds to loopback by default. Do not set
\fBWORDSWIFT_HOST=0.0.0.0\fR without a reverse proxy that enforces
authentication: the write endpoints (\fBPOST /indexes\fR,
\fBPOST /indexes/{name}/documents\fR, \fBDELETE /indexes/{name}\fR) would be open
to anyone able to reach the host.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/wordswift.service
The unit file.
.TP
.I /etc/wordswift/wordswift.env
Optional overrides; a conffile.
.TP
.I /var/lib/wordswift
Index storage: \fImanifest.json\fR plus a \fI<name>_index\fR and \fI<name>_ft\fR
LevelDB directory per index. Mode 0750, owned by \fBwordswift\fR. Removed on
package purge, preserved on plain removal.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/sysusers.d/wordswift.conf
Creates the \fBwordswift\fR system user and group.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/tmpfiles.d/wordswift.conf
Creates the state and config directories with correct ownership.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wordswift "(1), " systemd.service "(5), " systemd.exec "(5), "
.BR tmpfiles.d "(5), " sysusers.d "(5), "
.BR journalctl (1)
.PP
Upstream documentation: <https://github.com/djinn/WordSwift>.
